# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.2 have "annexes → land_subject"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "by uniting himself to the commonwealth he annexes and submits to the community the possessions he has or shall acquire" is true, which the second allows
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "by uniting himself to the commonwealth he annexes and submits to the community the possessions he has or shall acquire" is true and "his possessions become subject to the government and dominion of that commonwealth as long as it hath a being" is false. Is S4, "By the same act therefore, whereby any one unites his person, which was before free, to any commonwealth, by the same he unites his possessions, which were before free, to it also; and they become, both of them, person and possession, subject to the government and dominion of that commonwealth, as long as it hath a being.", true then?
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r1 has "land_subject ∧ enjoys → submits"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.2 and locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "his possessions become subject to the government and dominion of that commonwealth as long as it hath a being" and "someone thenceforth enjoys, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherwise, any part of the land so annexed" are true, which the second allows
locke-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-2.logic.r1, to: Suppose "his possessions become subject to the government and dominion of that commonwealth as long as it hath a being" and "someone thenceforth enjoys, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherwise, any part of the land so annexed" are true and "the one who enjoys the land takes it with the condition of submitting to the government of the commonwealth as far forth as any subject of it" is false. Is S5, "Whoever therefore, from thenceforth, by inheritance, purchase, permission, or otherways, enjoys any part of the land, so annexed to, and under the government of that commonwealth, must take it with the condition it is under; that is, of submitting to the government of the commonwealth, under whose jurisdiction it is, as far forth as any subject of it.", true then?
locke-treatise-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: locke-treatise-2.logic.r1 has "¬land_exempt → annexes"; locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.2 has "¬annexes → land_exempt" and "land_subject ∧ enjoys → submits"; they differ when only "his land, whose property is to be regulated by the laws of the society, is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government", "by uniting himself to the commonwealth he annexes and submits to the community the possessions he has or shall acquire", "his possessions become subject to the government and dominion of that commonwealth as long as it hath a being" and "someone thenceforth enjoys, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherwise, any part of the land so annexed" are true, which the first allows; locke-treatise-2.logic.r1.3 has "¬land_exempt → annexes" and "annexes → land_subject" and "land_subject ∧ enjoys → submits"
